      "author": "Hollowmere team",
      "story": "Reverend Joan Haskett rang St. Ansel's bell for anyone left in the valley, and they came: Rosa from the Mile 9 with her regulars, the Okafors off the logging road, the Merrow kids for a night. She chained the front doors and let people in by the vestry past the graves, kept the town's food pantry in the basement under her own key, and bedded forty-one down in the fellowship hall. On the seventh night the vigil filled the pews. A roadblock worker in the back row had hidden a bite, and by midnight half the congregation had stopped singing. The families below barricaded the pantry stair from underneath and bolted the side door. Rosa was already coughing, and asked to be locked in the pantry with the food; Joan did it, climbed to the belfry with the key and rang the bell until the rope parted, to draw the turned out of her church. Some went. The rest are still in the pews, and the fever was already down in the fellowship hall.",
